Output~2025-04-24 16:42:12.137270: [RequestOutput(request_id=316, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Write a scientific explanation for why the sky is blue. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 9842, 264, 12624, 16540, 369, 3249, 279, 13180, 374, 6437, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="The sky appears blue to our eyes because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering, which is the scattering of light by small particles or molecules in the atmosphere. This scattering effect is responsible for the blue color of the sky during the daytime.\n\nWhen sunlight enters the Earth's atmosphere, it encounters tiny molecules of gases such as nitrogen (N2) and oxygen (O2). These molecules are much smaller than the wavelength of light, so they scatter the shorter, blue wavelengths more efficiently than the longer, red wavelengths. This is known as Rayleigh scattering, named after the British physicist Lord Rayleigh, who first described the phenomenon in the late 19th century.\n\nAs a result of this scattering, the blue light is distributed throughout the atmosphere, giving the sky its blue appearance. The shorter wavelengths of light, such as violet and ultraviolet, are also scattered, but they are absorbed by the atmosphere and do not reach our eyes.\n\nThe color of the sky can vary depending on a number of factors, including the time of day, the amount of cloud cover, and the amount of dust and pollution in the atmosphere. During sunrise and sunset, the sky can take on hues of red and orange due to the scattering of longer wavelengths of light by atmospheric particles. At night, the sky can appear dark, but can also be illuminated by the moon, stars, and artificial light sources.\n\nIn addition to Rayleigh scattering, there are other factors that can affect the color of the sky, such as Mie scattering, which is the scattering of light by larger particles such as dust, pollen, and water droplets. This type of scattering can give the sky a more hazy or orange appearance.\n\nIn summary, the sky appears blue because of the scattering of sunlight by the small molecules of gases in the Earth's atmosphere, a phenomenon known as Rayleigh scattering. This scattering effect is responsible for the blue color of the sky during the daytime, and can be affected by a number of factors, including the time of day, cloud cover, and atmospheric conditions.", token_ids=[791, 13180, 8111, 6437, 311, 1057, 6548, 1606, 315, 264, 25885, 2663, 13558, 64069, 72916, 11, 902, 374, 279, 72916, 315, 3177, 555, 2678, 19252, 477, 35715, 304, 279, 16975, 13, 1115, 72916, 2515, 374, 8647, 369, 279, 6437, 1933, 315, 279, 13180, 2391, 279, 62182, 382, 4599, 40120, 29933, 279, 9420, 596, 16975, 11, 433, 35006, 13987, 35715, 315, 45612, 1778, 439, 47503, 320, 45, 17, 8, 323, 24463, 320, 46, 17, 570, 4314, 35715, 527, 1790, 9333, 1109, 279, 46406, 315, 3177, 11, 779, 814, 45577, 279, 24210, 11, 6437, 93959, 810, 30820, 1109, 279, 5129, 11, 2579, 93959, 13, 1115, 374, 3967, 439, 13558, 64069, 72916, 11, 7086, 1306, 279, 8013, 83323, 10425, 13558, 64069, 11, 889, 1176, 7633, 279, 25885, 304, 279, 3389, 220, 777, 339, 9478, 382, 2170, 264, 1121, 315, 420, 72916, 11, 279, 6437, 3177, 374, 4332, 6957, 279, 16975, 11, 7231, 279, 13180, 1202, 6437, 11341, 13, 578, 24210, 93959, 315, 3177, 11, 1778, 439, 80836, 323, 37232, 85311, 11, 527, 1101, 38067, 11, 719, 814, 527, 42101, 555, 279, 16975, 323, 656, 539, 5662, 1057, 6548, 382, 791, 1933, 315, 279, 13180, 649, 1