Output~2025-04-24 16:42:52.091382: [RequestOutput(request_id=330, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Explain why computational models can be used in analysing text. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 83017, 3249, 55580, 4211, 649, 387, 1511, 304, 22209, 287, 1495, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Computational models can be used in analyzing text because they offer a powerful tool for extracting insights and meaning from large amounts of unstructured data. These models are designed to mimic human-like understanding of language, allowing them to identify patterns, relationships, and trends in text data that may not be immediately apparent to humans.\n\nOne of the primary advantages of using computational models in text analysis is their ability to process and analyze large volumes of data quickly and efficiently. This is particularly useful for tasks such as sentiment analysis, entity recognition, and topic modeling, where the sheer scale of the data can be overwhelming for human analysts.\n\nComputational models can also be trained on large datasets to learn patterns and relationships that are specific to a particular domain or industry. For example, a model trained on a dataset of medical research papers can be used to identify key concepts and relationships in the field of medicine, allowing researchers to quickly and accurately identify relevant information.\n\nAnother benefit of computational models is their ability to handle ambiguity and uncertainty in text data. Human analysts may struggle to interpret ambiguous or unclear language, but computational models can be designed to handle these complexities using techniques such as natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning.\n\nFinally, computational models can be used to automate many tasks in text analysis, freeing up human analysts to focus on higher-level tasks such as interpretation and decision-making. For example, a model can be used to automatically extract key phrases and sentences from a large document, allowing an analyst to quickly identify the main points and themes.\n\nOverall, computational models offer a powerful tool for analyzing text data, allowing analysts to extract insights and meaning from large volumes of unstructured data quickly and efficiently.', token_ids=[59122, 1697, 4211, 649, 387, 1511, 304, 42118, 1495, 1606, 814, 3085, 264, 8147, 5507, 369, 60508, 26793, 323, 7438, 505, 3544, 15055, 315, 653, 52243, 828, 13, 4314, 4211, 527, 6319, 311, 56459, 3823, 12970, 8830, 315, 4221, 11, 10923, 1124, 311, 10765, 12912, 11, 12135, 11, 323, 18845, 304, 1495, 828, 430, 1253, 539, 387, 7214, 10186, 311, 12966, 382, 4054, 315, 279, 6156, 22934, 315, 1701, 55580, 4211, 304, 1495, 6492, 374, 872, 5845, 311, 1920, 323, 24564, 3544, 27378, 315, 828, 6288, 323, 30820, 13, 1115, 374, 8104, 5505, 369, 9256, 1778, 439, 27065, 6492, 11, 5502, 18324, 11, 323, 8712, 34579, 11, 1405, 279, 33638, 5569, 315, 279, 828, 649, 387, 22798, 369, 3823, 31499, 382, 59122, 1697, 4211, 649, 1101, 387, 16572, 389, 3544, 30525, 311, 4048, 12912, 323, 12135, 430, 527, 3230, 311, 264, 4040, 8106, 477, 5064, 13, 1789, 3187, 11, 264, 1646, 16572, 389, 264, 10550, 315, 6593, 3495, 16064, 649, 387, 1511, 311, 10765, 1401, 19476, 323, 12135, 304, 279, 2115, 315, 16088, 11, 10923, 12074, 311, 6288, 323, 30357, 10765, 9959, 2038, 382, 14364, 8935, 315, 55580, 4211, 374, 872, 5845, 311, 3790, 72868, 323, 27924, 304, 1495, 828, 13, 11344, 31499, 1253, 14993, 31