Output~2025-04-24 16:49:39.354212: [RequestOutput(request_id=484, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Write a Python function that takes two integers and returns their least common multiple. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 9842, 264, 13325, 734, 430, 5097, 1403, 26864, 323, 4780, 872, 3325, 4279, 5361, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="Here is a Python function that calculates the least common multiple (LCM) of two integers:\n```\ndef lcm(a, b):\n    def gcd(a, b):\n        while b:\n            a, b = b, a % b\n        return a\n    return abs(a*b) // gcd(a, b)\n```\nHere's an explanation of how the function works:\n\n1. The `gcd` function calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n2. The `lcm` function calculates the LCM of two numbers by multiplying them together and dividing by their GCD.\n3. The `abs` function is used to ensure that the result is always positive, since the GCD can be negative.\n\nYou can test the function with some examples:\n```\nprint(lcm(12, 15))  # Output: 60\nprint(lcm(24, 30))  # Output: 120\nprint(lcm(7, 11))   # Output: 77\n```\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.", token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 13325, 734, 430, 48517, 279, 3325, 4279, 5361, 320, 8724, 44, 8, 315, 1403, 26864, 512, 14196, 4077, 755, 87179, 2948, 11, 293, 997, 262, 711, 45958, 2948, 11, 293, 997, 286, 1418, 293, 512, 310, 264, 11, 293, 284, 293, 11, 264, 1034, 293, 198, 286, 471, 