Output~2025-04-24 17:00:39.822037: [RequestOutput(request_id=765, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task, paired with an input that provides further context. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Design an algorithm to sort a given list. ### Input: [4, 1, 6, 5, 2] ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 11, 35526, 449, 459, 1988, 430, 5825, 4726, 2317, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 7127, 459, 12384, 311, 3460, 264, 2728, 1160, 13, 17010, 5688, 25, 510, 19, 11, 220, 16, 11, 220, 21, 11, 220, 20, 11, 220, 17, 60, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="Here is a simple algorithm to sort the given list using the Bubble Sort algorithm:\n\n```\ndef bubble_sort(lst):\n    n = len(lst)\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(0, n - i - 1):\n            if lst[j] > lst[j + 1]:\n                lst[j], lst[j + 1] = lst[j + 1], lst[j]\n    return lst\n\ninput_list = [4, 1, 6, 5, 2]\nsorted_list = bubble_sort(input_list)\nprint(sorted_list)\n```\n\nWhen you run this code, it will output: `[1, 2, 4, 5, 6]`, which is the sorted version of the input list.\n\nHere's how the algorithm works:\n\n1. The outer loop (`for i in range(n)`) runs `n` times, where `n` is the length of the list.\n2. The inner loop (`for j in range(0, n - i - 1)`) compares each pair of adjacent elements in the list.\n3. If a pair of elements is in the wrong order (i.e., the first element is greater than the second), the algorithm swaps them.\n4. The algorithm repeats steps 2 and 3 until the end of the list is reached.\n5. The outer loop repeats steps 2-4 until no more swaps are needed, indicating that the list is sorted.\n\nThis algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2), making it less efficient for large lists. However, it is simple to implement and can be useful for small lists or educational purposes.", token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 4382, 12384, 311, 3460, 279, 2728, 1160, 1701, 279, 54424, 16347, 12384, 1473, 14196, 4077, 755, 24529, 18942, 47146, 997, 262, 308, 284, 2479, 47146, 340, 262, 369, 602, 304, 2134, 1471, 997, 286, 369, 503, 304, 2134, 7, 15, 11, 308, 482, 602, 482, 220, 16, 997, 310, 422, 19376, 3894, 60, 871, 19376, 3894, 489, 220, 16, 10556, 394, 19376, 3894, 1145, 19376, 3894, 489, 220, 16, 60, 284, 19376, 3894, 489, 220, 16, 1145, 19376, 3894, 933, 262, 471, 19376, 271, 1379, 2062, 284, 510, 19, 11, 220, 16, 11, 220, 21, 11, 220, 20, 11, 220, 17, 933, 29398, 2062, 284, 24529, 18942, 5498, 2062, 340, 1374, 45486, 2062, 340, 14196, 19884, 4599, 499, 1629, 420, 2082, 11, 433, 690, 2612, 25, 78744, 16, 11, 220, 17, 11, 220, 19, 11, 220, 20, 11, 220, 21, 60, 7964, 902, 374, 279, 10839, 2373, 315, 279, 1988, 1160, 382, 8586, 596, 1268, 279, 12384, 4375, 1473, 16, 13, 578, 16335, 6471, 29754, 2000, 602, 304, 2134, 1471, 8, 33981, 8640, 1595, 77, 63, 3115, 11, 1405, 1595, 77, 63, 374, 279, 3160, 315, 279, 1160, 627, 17, 13, 578, 9358, 6471, 29754, 2000, 503, 304, 2134, 7, 15, 11, 308, 482, 602, 482, 220, 16, 8, 33981, 43565, 1855, 6857, 315, 24894, 5540, 304, 279, 1160, 627, 18, 13, 1442, 264, 6857, 315, 5540, 374, 304, 279, 5076, 2015, 320, 72, 1770, 2637, 279, 1176, 2449, 374, 7191, 1109, 279, 2132, 705, 279, 12384, 74251, 1124, 627, 19, 13, 578, 12384, 44694, 7504, 220, 17, 323, 220, 18, 3156, 279, 842, 315, 279, 1160, 374, 8813, 627, 20, 13, 578, 16335, 6471, 44694, 7504, 220, 17, 12, 19, 3156, 912, 810, 74251, 527, 4460, 11, 19392, 430, 279, 1160, 374, 10839, 382, 2028, 12384, 706, 264, 892, 23965, 315, 507, 1471, 61, 17, 705, 3339, 433, 2753, 11297, 369, 3544, 11725, 13, 4452, 11, 433, 374, 4382, 311, 4305, 323,