Output~2025-04-24 17:08:37.873591: [RequestOutput(request_id=961, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: generate an algorithm to find the first common ancestor of two nodes in a binary tree. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 7068, 459, 12384, 311, 1505, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 315, 1403, 7954, 304, 264, 8026, 5021, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text="Here is an algorithm to find the first common ancestor of two nodes in a binary tree:\n\n**Algorithm:**\n\n1. Start at the root node of the binary tree.\n2. Compare the current node's value with the values of the two nodes you're looking for (let's call them `node1` and `node2`).\n3. If the current node's value is equal to either `node1` or `node2`, return the current node as the first common ancestor.\n4. If the current node's value is not equal to either `node1` or `node2`, recursively call the algorithm on the left subtree (if it exists) and the right subtree (if it exists).\n5. If the left subtree returns a non-null result, return that result as the first common ancestor.\n6. If the right subtree returns a non-null result, return that result as the first common ancestor.\n7. If both subtrees return null, return null as the first common ancestor.\n\n**Pseudocode:**\n```\nfunction findFirstCommonAncestor(root, node1, node2) {\n  if (root == null) {\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  if (root.value == node1 || root.value == node2) {\n    return root;\n  }\n\n  leftResult = findFirstCommonAncestor(root.left, node1, node2);\n  rightResult = findFirstCommonAncestor(root.right, node1, node2);\n\n  if (leftResult != null) {\n    return leftResult;\n  } else if (rightResult != null) {\n    return rightResult;\n  } else {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n```\n**Time complexity:** O(h), where h is the height of the binary tree.\n\n**Space complexity:** O(1), since we only use a constant amount of space to store the recursive function calls.\n\nThis algorithm works by traversing the binary tree in a depth-first manner, starting from the root node. It compares the current node's value with the values of the two nodes we're looking for, and recursively calls itself on the left and right subtrees if necessary. The first node that returns a non-null result is the first common ancestor of the two nodes. If both subtrees return null, it means that the two nodes are not in the same subtree, and we return null as the first common ancestor.", token_ids=[8586, 374, 459, 12384, 311, 1505, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 315, 1403, 7954, 304, 264, 8026, 5021, 1473, 334, 28947, 25, 57277, 16, 13, 5256, 520, 279, 3789, 2494, 315, 279, 8026, 5021, 627, 17, 13, 24702, 279, 1510, 2494, 596, 907, 449, 279, 2819, 315, 279, 1403, 7954, 499, 2351, 3411, 369, 320, 1169, 596, 1650, 1124, 1595, 3593, 16, 63, 323, 1595, 3593, 17, 63, 4390, 18, 13, 1442, 279, 1510, 2494, 596, 907, 374, 6273, 311, 3060, 1595, 3593, 16, 63, 477, 1595, 3593, 17, 7964, 471, 279, 1510, 2494, 439, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 627, 19, 13, 1442, 279, 1510, 2494, 596, 907, 374, 539, 6273, 311, 3060, 1595, 3593, 16, 63, 477, 1595, 3593, 17, 7964, 53947, 1650, 279, 12384, 389, 279, 2163, 54564, 320, 333, 433, 6866, 8, 323, 279, 1314, 54564, 320, 333, 433, 6866, 4390, 20, 13, 1442, 279, 2163, 54564, 4780, 264, 2536, 61441, 1121, 11, 471, 430, 1121, 439, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 627, 21, 13, 1442, 279, 1314, 54564, 4780, 264, 2536, 61441, 1121, 11, 471, 430, 1121, 439, 279, 1176, 4279, 46831, 627, 22, 13, 1442, 2225, 42129, 8016, 471, 854, 11, 471, 85