Output~2025-04-24 17:18:09.293361: [RequestOutput(request_id=1227, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Write a bash script to find all json files recursively inside a directory and output their names and size. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 9842, 264, 28121, 5429, 311, 1505, 682, 3024, 3626, 53947, 4871, 264, 6352, 323, 2612, 872, 5144, 323, 1404, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a bash script that finds all JSON files recursively inside a directory and outputs their names and sizes:\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\n\nfind . -type f -name "*.json" -exec stat -c "%n %s" {} \\;\n```\nLet me explain what this script does:\n\n* `find`: This command is used to search for files recursively.\n* `.`: This is the current directory, which is the starting point for the search.\n* `-type f`: This option tells `find` to only consider files (not directories).\n* `-name "*.json"`: This option specifies the file name pattern to search for. In this case, we\'re looking for files with the extension `.json`.\n* `-exec`: This option runs a command on each file found.\n* `stat -c "%n %s"`: This command runs the `stat` command on each file, with the `-c` option specifying the format of the output. The format string `"%n %s"` tells `stat` to output the file name (`%n`) and size (`%s`) in bytes.\n* `{}`: This is a placeholder for the file name, which is replaced by `find` with the actual file name.\n* `\\;`: This is the terminator of the `-exec` command. It\'s necessary to escape the semicolon (`;`) character, which has a special meaning in shell scripts.\n\nTo use this script, save it to a file (e.g., `find_json_files.sh`), make the file executable with `chmod +x find_json_files.sh`, and then run it with `./find_json_files.sh` in the directory you want to search.\n\nFor example, if you run the script in a directory containing the following JSON files:\n```\nfile1.json\nfile2.json\nsubdir/file3.json\nsubdir/subsubdir/file4.json\n```\nThe output would be:\n```\nfile1.json 1234\nfile2.json 5678\nsubdir/file3.json 9012\nsubdir/subsubdir/file4.json 3456\n```\nEach line shows the file name and its size in bytes.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 28121, 5429, 430, 14035, 682, 4823, 3626, 53947, 4871, 264, 6352, 323, 16674, 872, 5144, 323, 12562, 512, 74694, 47316, 198, 8872, 7006, 17587, 271, 3990, 662, 482, 1337, 282, 482, 609, 60228, 2285, 1, 482, 12010, 2863, 482, 66, 6090, 77, 1034, 82, 1, 4792, 1144, 280, 14196, 4077, 10267, 757, 10552, 1148, 420, 5429, 1587, 1473, 9, 1595, 3990, 45722, 1115, 3290, 374, 1511, 311, 2778, 369, 3626, 53947, 627, 9, 1595, 15254, 25, 1115, 374, 279, 1510, 6352, 11, 902, 374, 279, 6041, 1486, 369, 279, 2778, 627, 9, 94897, 1337, 282, 45722, 1115, 3072, 10975, 1595, 3990, 63, 311, 1193, 2980, 3626, 320, 1962, 29725, 4390, 9, 94897, 609, 60228, 2285, 41017, 25, 1115, 3072, 30202, 279, 1052, 836, 5497, 311, 2778, 369, 13, 763, 420, 1162, 11, 584, 2351, 3411, 369, 3626, 449, 279, 9070, 75190, 2285, 19154, 9, 94897, 12010, 45722, 1115, 3072, 8640, 264, 3290, 389, 1855, 1052, 1766, 627, 9, 1595, 10079, 482, 66, 6090, 77, 1034, 82, 41017, 25, 1115, 3290, 8640, 279, 1595, 10079, 63, 3290, 389, 1855, 1052, 11, 449, 279, 94897, 66, 63, 3072, 38938, 279, 3645, 315, 279, 2612, 13, 578, 3645, 925, 1595, 32491, 77, 1034, 82, 41017, 10975, 1595, 10079, 63, 311, 2612, 279, 1052, 836, 29754, 4, 77, 33981, 323, 1404, 29754, 4, 82, 33981, 304, 5943, 627, 9, 54792, 5658, 25, 1115, 374, 264, 6002, 369, 279, 1052, 836, 11, 902