Output~2025-04-24 17:50:03.294842: [RequestOutput(request_id=1944, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Create a function to check if a given year is a leap year. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 4324, 264, 734, 311, 1817, 422, 264, 2728, 1060, 374, 264, 32571, 1060, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a Python function that checks if a given year is a leap year:\n```\ndef is_leap_year(year):\n    """\n    Returns True if the given year is a leap year, False otherwise.\n    """\n    return year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)\n```\nHere\'s an explanation of the logic:\n\n* A year is a leap year if it is divisible by 4, except for years that are divisible by 100 but not by 400.\n* The `year % 4 == 0` part checks if the year is divisible by 4.\n* The `year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0` part checks if the year is not divisible by 100, or if it is divisible by 400. This is because years that are divisible by 100 but not by 400 are not leap years.\n\nYou can test this function with different years to see if it works correctly:\n```\nprint(is_leap_year(2020))  # True\nprint(is_leap_year(2019))  # False\nprint(is_leap_year(2000))  # True\nprint(is_leap_year(1900))  # False\n```\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 13325, 734, 430, 12621, 422, 264, 2728, 1060, 374, 264, 32571, 1060, 512, 14196, 4077, 755, 374, 12013, 391, 14987, 29381, 997, 262, 3270, 262, 5295, 3082, 422, 279,