Output~2025-04-06 23:50:52.081166: [RequestOutput(request_id=353, prompt='<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{"Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: How would you explain the concept of a \'memory leak\' to someone with no background in computing? ### Response:"}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n', prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 5018, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 2650, 1053, 499, 10552, 279, 7434, 315, 264, 364, 17717, 24237, 6, 311, 4423, 449, 912, 4092, 304, 25213, 30, 17010, 6075, 3047, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here\'s a response that explains the concept of a "memory leak" in a way that\'s easy to understand for someone with no background in computing:\n\n"A memory leak is like a leak in a water pipe. Imagine you\'re filling up a bucket with water, and you\'re using that water to water your plants. But, somehow, the water is slowly dripping out of the bucket and onto the floor, even though you\'re not turning on the faucet anymore. You\'re still using the same bucket, but the water level keeps going down, and you\'re not sure why.\n\nIn computing, memory is like that bucket. When a program runs, it uses a certain amount of memory to store information, like a virtual bucket. But sometimes, a program might not properly clean up after itself and release the memory it\'s using when it\'s done with it. This is like the water leak - the program is still using the memory, but it\'s not returning it to the "bucket" (the computer\'s memory pool).\n\nOver time, these small leaks can add up, and the program can start to use more and more memory, even if it\'s not actually doing anything. This can cause problems, like slowing down the computer or even making it crash. A memory leak is like a slow drain on the computer\'s resources, and it\'s something that programmers need to fix to keep their programs running smoothly and efficiently."', token_ids=[8586, 596, 264, 2077, 430, 15100, 279, 7434, 315, 264, 330, 17717, 24237, 1, 304, 264, 1648, 430, 596, 4228, 311, 3619, 369, 4423, 449, 912, 4092, 304, 25213, 1473, 30233, 5044, 24237, 374, 1093, 264, 24237, 304, 264, 3090, 13961, 13, 38891, 499, 2351, 21973, 709, 264, 15994, 449, 3090, 11, 323, 499, 2351, 1701, 430, 3090, 311, 3090, 701, 11012, 13, 2030, 11, 17354, 11, 279, 3090, 374, 14297, 77315, 704, 315, 279, 15994, 323, 8800, 279, 6558, 11, 1524, 3582, 499, 2351, 539, 13353, 389, 279, 72553, 14926, 13, 1472, 2351, 2103, 1701, 279, 1890, 15994, 11, 719, 279, 3090, 2237, 13912, 2133, 1523, 11, 323, 499, 2351, 539, 2771, 3249, 382, 644, 25213, 11, 5044, 374, 1093, 430, 15994, 13, 3277, 264, 2068, 8640, 11, 433, 5829, 264, 3738, 3392, 315, 5044, 311, 3637, 2038, 11, 1093, 264, 4200, 15994, 13, 2030, 7170, 11, 264, 2068, 2643, 539, 10489, 4335, 709, 1306, 5196, 323, 4984, 279, 5044, 433, 596, 1701, 994, 433, 596, 2884, 449, 433, 13, 1115, 374, 1093, 279, 3090, 24237, 482, 279, 2068, 374, 2103, 1701, 279, 5044, 11, 719, 433, 596, 539, 13758, 433, 311, 279, 330, 31510, 1, 320, 1820, 6500, 596, 5044, 7463, 3677, 1959, 892, 11, 1521, 2678, 37796, 649, 923, 709, 11, 323, 279, 2068, 649, 1212, 311, 1005, 810, 323, 810, 5044, 11, 1524, 422, 433, 596, 539, 3604, 3815, 4205, 13, 1115, 649, 5353, 5435, 11, 1093, 48408, 1523, 279, 6500, 477, 1524, 3339, 433, 10121, 13, 362, 5044, 24237, 374, 1093, 264, 6435, 24659, 389, 279, 6500, 596, 5070, 11, 323, 433, 596, 2555, 430, 55946, 1205, 311, 5155, 311, 2567, 872, 7620, 4401, 39511, 323, 30820, 1210, 128009], cumulative_logprob=None, logprobs=None, finish_reason=stop, stop_reason=None)], finished=True, metrics=None, lora_request=None, num_cached_tokens=None, multi_modal_plac