Output~2025-04-06 23:57:27.124980: [RequestOutput(request_id=511, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Write a C++ function for calculating the approximate area of a circle. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 9842, 264, 356, 1044, 734, 369, 38714, 279, 45968, 3158, 315, 264, 12960, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='Here is a C++ function that calculates the approximate area of a circle:\n```\n#include <cmath>\n\ndouble calculateCircleArea(double radius) {\n    return M_PI * pow(radius, 2);\n}\n```\nThis function takes a `double` value for the radius of the circle as input, and returns the approximate area of the circle using the formula `A = πr^2`, where `A` is the area and `r` is the radius.\n\nNote: The `M_PI` constant is a mathematical constant that represents the value of pi (π). It is not a standard C++ constant, but it is available in many C++ implementations. If your compiler does not support `M_PI`, you can use a constant value for pi, such as `3.14159`.', token_ids=[8586, 374, 264, 356, 1044, 734, 430, 48517, 279, 45968, 3158, 315, 264, 12960, 512, 14196, 4077, 1085, 366, 34825, 1363, 4429, 11294, 26264, 8900, 9078, 10801, 8, 341, 262, 471, 386, 20825, 353, 7019, 62122, 11, 220, 17, 317, 534, 14196, 4077, 2028, 734, 5097, 264, 1595, 4429, 63, 907, 369, 279, 10801, 315, 279, 12960, 439, 1988, 11, 323, 4780, 279, 45968, 3158, 315, 279, 12960, 1701, 279, 15150, 1595, 32, 284, 52845, 81, 61, 17, 7964, 1405, 1595, 32, 63, 374, 279, 3158, 323,