Output~2025-04-07 00:31:24.496235: [RequestOutput(request_id=1246, prompt="<|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\n{'Below is an instruction that describes a task. Write a response that appropriately completes the request. ### Instruction: Describe the history of humans in space. ### Response:'}<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\n", prompt_token_ids=[128000, 128000, 128006, 882, 128007, 271, 13922, 39314, 374, 459, 7754, 430, 16964, 264, 3465, 13, 9842, 264, 2077, 430, 36001, 45695, 279, 1715, 13, 17010, 30151, 25, 61885, 279, 3925, 315, 12966, 304, 3634, 13, 17010, 6075, 4989, 92, 128009, 128006, 78191, 128007, 271], encoder_prompt=None, encoder_prompt_token_ids=None, prompt_logprobs=None, outputs=[CompletionOutput(index=0, text='The history of humans in space began on April 12, 1961, when Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first human to journey into outer space. Gagarin\'s flight aboard the Vostok 1 spacecraft lasted 108 minutes, and he completed one orbit of the Earth at an altitude of 327 kilometers (203 miles).\n\nThe United States soon followed suit, with astronaut Alan Shepard becoming the first American in space on May 5, 1961, aboard the Freedom 7 spacecraft. Shepard\'s 15-minute suborbital flight reached an altitude of 115 kilometers (72 miles).\n\nThe first American in orbit was John Glenn, who flew aboard the Friendship 7 spacecraft on February 20, 1962. Glenn\'s mission, Mercury-Atlas 6, lasted for nearly five hours and four orbits of the Earth.\n\nThe Soviet Union continued to push the boundaries of space exploration, with cosmonaut Valentina Tereshkova becoming the first woman in space on June 16, 1963. Tereshkova\'s Vostok 6 mission lasted for almost three days, during which she completed 48 orbits of the Earth.\n\nThe United States and the Soviet Union continued to engage in a space race, with both countries sending humans to space and conducting scientific experiments. The Apollo program, launched by NASA in 1961, aimed to land humans on the Moon. On July 20, 1969, NASA astronauts Neil Armstrong and Edwin "Buzz" Aldrin became the first humans to set foot on the Moon\'s surface during the Apollo 11 mission.\n\nSince the early days of space exploration, humans have continued to venture into space, with numerous space stations, shuttles, and spacecraft being launched. The International Space Station, a collaborative project between space agencies around the world, has been continuously occupied by astronauts and cosmonauts since 2000.\n\nIn recent years, private companies such as SpaceX and Blue Origin have also entered the space industry, with SpaceX\'s Dragon spacecraft and Blue Origin\'s New Shepard rocket carrying both people and cargo to space. NASA\'s Artemis program aims to return humans to the Moon by 2024 and establish a sustainable presence on the lunar surface.\n\nToday, humans continue to push the boundaries of space exploration, with ongoing missions to the International Space Station, the Moon, and beyond. The history of humans in space is a testament to human ingenuity, curiosity, and the desire to explore and understand the vastness of the universe.', token_ids=[791, 3925, 315, 12966, 304, 3634, 6137, 389, 5936, 220, 717, 11, 220, 5162, 16, 11, 994, 19953, 8119, 1677, 2784, 86873, 480, 38812, 258, 6244, 279, 1176, 3823, 311, 11879, 1139, 16335, 3634, 13, 480, 38812, 258, 596, 11213, 37606, 279, 650, 537, 564, 220, 16, 42640, 36513, 220, 6640, 4520, 11, 323, 568, 8308, 832, 27605, 315, 279, 9420, 520, 459, 36958, 315, 220, 13817, 41668, 320, 9639, 8931, 3677, 791, 3723, 4273, 5246, 8272, 7937, 11, 449, 47733, 26349, 72919, 10671, 279, 1176, 3778, 304, 3634, 389, 3297, 220, 20, 11, 220, 5162, 16, 11, 37606, 279, 25320, 220, 22, 42640, 13, 72919, 596, 220, 868, 24401, 1207, 30986, 2223, 11213, 8813, 459, 36958, 315, 220, 7322, 41668, 320, 5332, 8931, 3677, 791, 1176, 3778, 304, 27605, 574, 3842, 40208, 11, 889, 32122, 37606, 279, 73169, 220, 22, 426